// Gobra contracts for MemStore — F1, F2, F3, F5 (Go scope), F6, F9.
//
// RETARGETED FOR THE FLAT RESHAPE (S-12). Everything below used to describe
// the nested containers `follows map[string]map[string]bool` and
// `byAuthor map[string][]dom.Tweet`. Those are gone; see the package comment
// in memstore.go for the shapes that replaced them. The predicate had to be
// retargeted because it still named `s.byAuthor`, a field that no longer
// exists — which is what made the whole package fail to type-check.
//
// F5 caveat, unchanged: the mutex specs establish data-race freedom under
// partial correctness only. Deadlock-freedom and starvation-freedom are out of
// scope and are covered instead by `go test -race`.
//
// # What LockP() now carries
//
// LockP() is no longer purely a permission token. It also carries THE APPEND
// LOG INVARIANT:
//
//	for all positions i < j:  tweets[i].ID < tweets[j].ID
//	                     and  tweets[i].CreatedAt <= tweets[j].CreatedAt
//
// This is the monotonicity lemma from D9, stated as a datatype invariant
// rather than as a comment. Putting it in the predicate is what turns F2 from
// an assumption into a theorem:
//
//   - `New()` folds LockP() over a nil log, where the quantifier is vacuous.
//   - `PutTweet` re-establishes it on every append. Its guard rejects any
//     tweet whose id does not strictly exceed, or whose timestamp falls below,
//     the current last entry. Gobra checks that this guard is exactly strong
//     enough to restore the invariant — it is not taken on trust.
//   - `HomeTimeline` consumes it to discharge an F2 POSTCONDITION.
//
// # F2 is proved, and no sort specification was needed
//
// `HomeTimeline` now carries
//
//	ensures forall a, b :: a < b < len(out) ==>
//	          out[a].CreatedAt >  out[b].CreatedAt ||
//	         (out[a].CreatedAt == out[b].CreatedAt && out[a].ID > out[b].ID)
//
// and Gobra discharges it. This is the obligation the upstream sidecar
// recorded as unreachable: it said F2 "is blocked on a verified sort spec,
// tracked for the S3P6 stub-discharge follow-up stream." That blocker is not
// resolved so much as DELETED — the code performs no sort, so no sort
// specification is owed. The proof is the backwards walk over a log the
// invariant already orders. `sortTimeline` is gone and does not come back.
//
// # F1 IS NOW PROVED (S-13)
//
// The previous text here read "F1 is still NOT proved", and gave the reason:
// "expressing it needs the returned elements to be related back to their
// source positions in `s.tweets` — a ghost sequence of log indices threaded
// through the loop — which is a larger change than retargeting this file."
//
// The diagnosis was right and the size estimate was wrong. The ghost sequence
// is `src`, declared in `HomeTimeline` and extended by one line inside the
// loop, and it buys three postconditions rather than one:
//
//	F1   every returned tweet is authored by `user` or by someone `user`
//	     follows -- and the visibility test does not even need `src`, only a
//	     loop invariant over `buf`;
//	no fabrication  every returned tweet is some entry of the log;
//	no loss         when the walk reached the bottom of the log, every visible
//	                entry under the cursor is on the page.
//
// The last two are what turn a one-sided containment into a response
// commutation. All three are canaried: dropping one visible tweet from the
// page, or deleting the visibility filter, each fails a named invariant.
//
// # Which trusted shims came back, and why
//
// The reshape retired six shims. Three of them stay retired; three had to be
// reintroduced, and NOT for the reason the originals existed. Measured
// against this Gobra build (1.1-SNAPSHOT, image sha256:2ef080cc):
//
//	putFollowEdge   RETIRED. A flat map write `s.follows[f] = true` verifies.
//	gatherTimeline  RETIRED. The backwards indexed walk verifies.
//	sortTimeline    RETIRED. There is no sort to specify.
//
//	deleteFollowEdge  BACK. Gobra has no model for the `delete` builtin at
//	                  all — flat or nested, it reports "got unknown
//	                  identifier delete".
//	appendTweet       BACK. Gobra's `append` takes a permission as its first
//	                  argument; Go's two-argument form is a type error, and
//	                  the permission form is not valid Go.
//	iterFollows       BACK. Gobra cannot verify `range` over a map — even an
//	                  empty-bodied `for range m` under full permission fails.
//
// The first three were about nested permission, which flattening fixes. The
// last three are front-end gaps in the verifier, which flattening cannot
// touch. Each shim's own comment in memstore.go records the exact diagnostic.

package store

import "github.com/michaellady/twitter-port-matrix-impl-go/internal/dom"

// LockP is the lock invariant for *MemStore: permission to the fields the
// `mu` mutex protects, PLUS the append-log ordering invariant that F2 is
// derived from. `mu` is a value-typed `sync.RWMutex` and `stubs/sync` declares
// no permission tokens on it, so no field permission for `mu` is asserted
// beyond `acc(&s.mu)`.
//
// The quantifier is the whole point. Without it the log's ordering would be a
// property maintained by PutTweet's guard but visible to no other method, and
// HomeTimeline could prove nothing about the order it returns.
pred (s *MemStore) LockP() {
	acc(&s.mu) &&
	acc(&s.users) && acc(s.users) &&
	acc(&s.follows) && acc(s.follows) &&
	acc(&s.tweets) && acc(s.tweets) &&
	(forall i, j int :: 0 <= i && i < j && j < len(s.tweets) ==>
		s.tweets[i].ID < s.tweets[j].ID && s.tweets[i].CreatedAt <= s.tweets[j].CreatedAt) &&
	// R5 (follows axis). Needed so `domain(s.follows)` IS the edge set rather
	// than merely a superset of it. Go maps a missing key to the zero value,
	// so without this a key present-but-false would make `Follows()` answer
	// false for an edge that `AbsFollows()` says is present -- the response
	// and state halves of the obligation would disagree. Nothing in the
	// package ever stores false; this invariant is what makes that a checked
	// fact instead of a reading of the code.
	(forall f dom.Follow :: f in domain(s.follows) ==> s.follows[f])
}

// =============================================================================
// R5 — the abstraction function.
// =============================================================================
//
// R4 says "this implementation satisfies property number N". R5 says something
// strictly stronger: there is a function abs from implementation states to
// S_obs states, and every operation commutes with it. That is what licenses
// "A and B are observationally equivalent", because both sides land on the
// same S_obs state after the same request sequence.
//
// abs is written here as a family of PURE GHOST PROJECTIONS, one per axis of
// the S_obs state, rather than as a single function returning a record: Gobra
// has no ghost struct literal that can be built inside a `pure` function
// without also owning the fields, and splitting by axis costs nothing because
// the obligation is stated per axis anyway.
//
// Each projection is CONCRETE, not uninterpreted. `AbsUsers()` really is
// `domain(s.users)`. That is the difference between stating the obligation and
// assuming it: an uninterpreted abs with axiomatised commutation clauses would
// verify trivially and prove nothing.

// AbsUsers is the users axis of abs: the set of registered handles.
// S_obs counterpart: the key set of `State.userByHandle`.
//
ghost
requires acc(s.LockP(), _)
decreases
pure func (s *MemStore) AbsUsers() (res set[string]) {
	return unfolding acc(s.LockP(), _) in domain(s.users)
}

// AbsFollows is the follows axis of abs: the set of directed edges.
// S_obs counterpart: the key set of `State.follows`.
ghost
requires acc(s.LockP(), _)
decreases
pure func (s *MemStore) AbsFollows() (res set[dom.Follow]) {
	return unfolding acc(s.LockP(), _) in domain(s.follows)
}

// AbsLogLen and AbsLogAt are the tweet-log axis of abs.
// S_obs counterpart: `State.tweets`, an append-ordered slice.
//
// The axis is exposed as a length plus an indexed projection rather than as a
// single `seq[dom.Tweet]` because Gobra 1.1-SNAPSHOT has no slice-to-sequence
// conversion in the ghost language -- there is no `toSeq(s.tweets)`. The
// pointwise form states exactly the same relation and costs one extra
// quantifier at each use site.
ghost
requires acc(s.LockP(), _)
decreases
pure func (s *MemStore) AbsLogLen() (res int) {
	return unfolding acc(s.LockP(), _) in len(s.tweets)
}

ghost
requires acc(s.LockP(), _)
requires 0 <= i && i < s.AbsLogLen()
decreases
pure func (s *MemStore) AbsLogAt(i int) (res dom.Tweet) {
	return unfolding acc(s.LockP(), _) in s.tweets[i]
}

// AbsAcceptsTweet is the accept predicate of the log axis, written against
// abs alone. It is S_obs's stepPostTweet author check, CONJOINED with the
// monotonicity guard PutTweet adds on top of S_obs.
//
// Keying the state commutation on this predicate rather than on `err == nil`
// is not a convenience. `err` is an interface value produced by a package
// level sentinel, and Gobra cannot prove those sentinels are non-nil (see the
// note on PutUser), so an `err`-keyed accept clause would be discharged for
// the wrong reason on the reject branches. Keying on abs and the request is
// also the literal shape of the R5 obligation:
//
//	abs(step_L(s, r)) = step_S(abs(s), r)
ghost
requires acc(s.LockP(), _)
decreases
pure func (s *MemStore) AbsAcceptsTweet(t dom.Tweet) (res bool) {
	return (t.Author in s.AbsUsers()) &&
		(s.AbsLogLen() == 0 ||
			(t.ID > s.AbsLogAt(s.AbsLogLen()-1).ID &&
				t.CreatedAt >= s.AbsLogAt(s.AbsLogLen()-1).CreatedAt))
}
